# Caddy site block for agent-home (FG-20). Append to /etc/caddy/Caddyfile on the
# prod box (alongside the existing `<dashboard-host>` dashboard block), then
# `sudo systemctl reload caddy`. Caddy fetches a Let's Encrypt cert for the
# subdomain automatically (HTTP-01), same as the dashboard. Replace
# `home.example.com` below with your agent-home host name.
#
# Replace the hostname with the subdomain you point at the box (A record ->
# 47.83.199.25, DNS-only / unproxied, matching the existing dashboard record).
#
# BFF note: the browser only ever talks to the agent-home Next server, which
# makes server-side calls to the Python AI layer (127.0.0.1:9119). So ALL public
# traffic on this origin goes to Next (127.0.0.1:3100) — including agent-home's
# own /api/session/* route handlers. Do NOT split /api/* to 9119 here; that
# would break the login/logout bridge routes. The Python API stays private.
#
# Port 3100 (not 3000): :3000 on this box is the WhatsApp bridge. Match the
# PORT set in the service's EnvironmentFile.
#
# app-mcp note: the `AppMcpBridge` (always-on UI bridge) and the opt-in
# Folder Bridge (`/files/bridge`) both dial a WebSocket on the standalone
# app-mcp service, loopback port 9221 (see app-mcp/README.md) — NOT the
# Next.js server on :3100. Both paths must be routed there explicitly
# (`reverse_proxy` forwards the `Connection: Upgrade` header automatically,
# same as any other proxied request); everything else on this origin,
# including agent-home's own /api/*, stays on :3100.

home.example.com {
    encode zstd gzip

    @app_mcp_ws path /app-mcp/ws /app-mcp/folders/ws
    handle @app_mcp_ws {
        reverse_proxy 127.0.0.1:9221
    }

    handle {
        reverse_proxy 127.0.0.1:3100
    }
}
